From ffe96b46670a300c0376ee516ded7f166ba4f224 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xffshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:28:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Keep the stride sway running in the air

The gate asked for dry land with the feet down (isWalking) or water
(isSwimming), so the feet leaving the ground took the sway off the head for
the length of the fall and then faded what was left of it out over the
twenty ticks after that - recentre is five percent a tick. On rolling
ground, where a path of any length is a run of one- and two-block drops,
that is most of a walk with a head that is not moving at all, and it is the
terrain switching it off rather than anything the head is doing.

Nothing about the air argues for it either. The game turns a fall by the
yaw exactly as it turns a walk, so a degree of drift in the yaw is a degree
of drift in the heading, which is the sentence the swim gate already makes
for its own axis; and the pitch steers nothing at all up there, so this gate
has no axis to leave alone the way that one does. The two states where the
look does steer the body keep their exclusions: water, where the pitch is
the depth, and an elytra, which is a control loop.

Deliberately without the speed test the other two carry: a walk stops when
the player does and the quieter sway takes over, but there is no standing
still in the air, and a straight drop with no horizontal speed to measure is
still the body moving under the head.

Validation: ./gradlew :test - 291 tests, 0 failures. The fuzz harness models
the stride gate on speed alone and cannot see the ground, so it models the
caller's ground and water halves; its note now says so, so the next reader
does not mistake those runs for coverage of the airborne half.
---
 .../java/baritone/behavior/LookBehavior.java  | 37 +++++++++++++++++--
 .../behavior/look/HumanlikeLookFuzzTest.java  |  6 +++
 2 files changed, 39 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/main/java/baritone/behavior/LookBehavior.java b/src/main/java/baritone/behavior/LookBehavior.java
index cd50dbc2..3a448ec9 100644
--- a/src/main/java/baritone/behavior/LookBehavior.java
+++ b/src/main/java/baritone/behavior/LookBehavior.java
@@ -929,9 +929,10 @@ public final class LookBehavior extends Behavior implements ILookBehavior {
     /**
      * Whether the head is ours to move, for the walk.
      * <p>
-     * Three conditions and all three are load bearing. The player has to be moving along - on foot or
-     * swimming, which are the same deal as far as the head is concerned (see {@link #isSwimming}) - because
-     * this is the stride, and standing still is a different gate and a quieter sway. Baritone has to be the one driving,
+     * Three conditions and all three are load bearing. The player has to be moving along - on foot, in
+     * water or in the air, which are the same deal as far as the head is concerned (see {@link
+     * #isSwimming} and {@link #isInTheAir}) - because this is the stride, and standing still is a
+     * different gate and a quieter sway. Baritone has to be the one driving,
      * because a head that moves on its own while a person is walking somewhere is not human, it is broken.
      * And nothing may be being clicked this tick, for the reason given on {@link #interacting()}: the trace
      * Baritone aims its own pickaxe and blocks along follows the rotation it last told the server, and a
@@ -945,7 +946,7 @@ public final class LookBehavior extends Behavior implements ILookBehavior {
      */
     private boolean canWander() {
         return this.target != null
-                && (this.isWalking() || this.isSwimming())
+                && (this.isWalking() || this.isSwimming() || this.isInTheAir())
                 && baritone.getPathingBehavior().isPathing()
                 && !this.interacting();
     }
@@ -990,6 +991,34 @@ public final class LookBehavior extends Behavior implements ILookBehavior {
         return dx * dx + dz * dz > IDLE_SPEED_SQ;
     }
 
+    /**
+     * Whether the player is off the ground and not being steered by the look: a fall, a jump, a climb,
+     * anything between the ground and the ground.
+     * <p>
+     * The feet leaving the ground used to switch the stride off - {@link #isWalking()} is dry land with the
+     * feet down, and {@link #isSwimming()} is water - so every drop of a block or two took the sway out of
+     * the head for the length of the fall and then faded the last of it out over the twenty ticks after
+     * that ({@link HeadWander#recentre} is five percent a tick). On rolling ground, where a path of any
+     * length is a run of little descents, that is most of a walk spent with a head that is not moving at
+     * all: the statue this sway exists to avoid, switched by the shape of the terrain rather than by
+     * anything the head was doing.
+     * <p>
+     * Nothing about the air argues for it. The game turns a fall by the yaw exactly as it turns a walk, so a
+     * degree of drift in the yaw is a degree of drift in the heading - the same sentence the swim gate makes
+     * - and the pitch steers nothing at all up there, which is the one place this gate is simpler than that
+     * one: there is no axis to leave alone. The two states that do steer the body by the look are water,
+     * where the pitch is the depth, and an elytra, which is a control loop; both are excluded here, and the
+     * elytra is excused from everything in this file for that reason.
+     * <p>
+     * Deliberately without the speed test the other two carry. A walk stops when the player does, and the
+     * quieter sway takes over; there is no standing still in the air, and a straight drop with no speed to
+     * measure on either horizontal axis is still the body moving under the head.
+     */
+    private boolean isInTheAir() {
+        return !ctx.player().onGround() && !ctx.player().isInWater() && !ctx.player().isFallFlying();
+    }
+
+
     /**
      * Whether an idle glance is appropriate right now.
      * <p>
diff --git a/src/test/java/baritone/behavior/look/HumanlikeLookFuzzTest.java b/src/test/java/baritone/behavior/look/HumanlikeLookFuzzTest.java
index c1f81e28..b8e10163 100644
--- a/src/test/java/baritone/behavior/look/HumanlikeLookFuzzTest.java
+++ b/src/test/java/baritone/behavior/look/HumanlikeLookFuzzTest.java
@@ -165,6 +165,12 @@ public class HumanlikeLookFuzzTest {
         // about under Baritone's control with nothing clicked, runs it at half under the glance. Nothing
         // being clicked is the gate they all share, because the rotation they are adding to is the one
         // Baritone traces its own pickaxe and blocks along.
+        //
+        // The body's half of the stride gate is modelled here as speed alone, which is what the two flags
+        // below can express: the caller (LookBehavior#canWander) counts a player who is off the ground as
+        // moving whatever their speed - a fall, a jump, a climb, where the yaw steers a heading just as it
+        // does on foot - and this harness has no ground to stand on. So its walking ticks model the caller's
+        // ground and water halves, and the airborne half is pinned by reading rather than by these runs.
         final boolean walkingSway = wanted != null && walking && pathing && !interacting;
         final boolean waitingSway = wanted != null && standingStill && inControl && !interacting;
         final boolean idleSway = wanted == null && inControl && standingStill && !interacting;
-- 
